2026-07-27T01:12:52.7227366Z           "after": "cd0a5029c565d638c1c72cea1ee747042ac24320",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7227690Z           "base_ref": null,
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7227979Z           "before": "0907a58f50a6c98d23ae466ed31c3a0c15264622",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7228592Z           "commits": [
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7228814Z             {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7229000Z               "author": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7229291Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7229611Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7229868Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7230138Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7230344Z               "committer": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7230581Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7230867Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7231110Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7231539Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7231739Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7232041Z               "id": "787ac09533d755f4c7a694582c6b87ecf4c03df1",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7240211Z               "message": "Give the shared-cache watch its own interval, defaulting to 30s\n\nThe epoch check was paced off FlushIntervalSeconds — 10 minutes by default —\npurely because a timer was already there. The two operations have nothing in\ncommon: a flush serialises the write bags and writes a file, this is a single\nRedis GET, a rounding error against the hundreds of L2 operations one\nvalidation already performs. Pacing the cheap health signal off the expensive\nwrite's interval left worst-case cold-L2 exposure at ten minutes for no reason.\n\nIt was also incoherent in the configuration now recommended. With\nCacheDir=none there is no flusher at all, yet recovery latency still came from\na key documented as \"how often newly-fetched results are written out\" —\ngoverning a subsystem that is not running. The lever that worked was the one\nthat looked irrelevant.\n\nSharedCacheWatchSeconds, default 30, cuts that exposure from ten minutes to\nhalf a minute for two extra GETs per instance per minute. 0 disables the\nwatch and says so at startup, since losing self-healing is a real cost rather\nthan a silent one; the enabled case logs its cadence too.\n\nFour tests, one of which had to be renamed after mutation testing showed it\ndid not mean what it said: asserting the interval defaults to 30 survived\nchanging the code fallback, because appsettings.json ships the value and the\nfallback never runs. It pins the shipped default, which is what an operator\ngets, and now says so.\n\nVerified end to end at a 2s cadence, with and without a disk tier: Redis\nemptied under a running instance, 169 entries republished from memory, no\nrestart and no validation traffic.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01611PR4pvQSGB1t9K3hNAwE",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7247312Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-26T08:16:26Z",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7247669Z               "tree_id": "f5a49bba96ffdca0e580b5814a2d42f9f529bcef",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7248430Z               "url": "https://github.com/pkinerd/ednsv/commit/787ac09533d755f4c7a694582c6b87ecf4c03df1"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7248920Z             },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7249096Z             {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7249305Z               "author": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7249567Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7249870Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7250123Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7250374Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7250568Z               "committer": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7250844Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7251135Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7251384Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7251614Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7251826Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7252121Z               "id": "e12ef815880932465da07cc6cbbc4673a918d3cd",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7264093Z               "message": "Correct the caching documentation against the code\n\nA full pass over every cache claim in the docs. The diagrams had drifted\nfurthest, and one section was actively wrong about behaviour.\n\nThe tier diagram never showed the Redis L2 at all — a whole tier missing from\nthe diagram whose job is to show the tiers — and had no shared-cache watch.\nThe ProbeCache flow diagram showed no L2 read on an L1 miss, no L2\nwrite-through, no per-entry TTL, and still said \"Export Log\". Both redrawn from\nthe code. Two edges in the new tier diagram were also imprecise on first pass:\nthe L2 warm happens via ProbeCache.Import, not from DiskCacheService directly,\nand the network -> Redis edge read as unconditional when it is gated by\nshouldPersist.\n\nThe shouldPersist table was silent about the shared L2, which that predicate\nalso gates — a transient error must not be published to peers any more than it\nshould reach disk.\n\nThe Service Cache Inventory asserted recheck behaviour that does not exist, and\nthat is the substantive finding here. It credited `_rcptCache` with\n`CacheDep.Rcpt` \"cleared via RemoveRcptEntries\" and `_relayCache` with\n`CacheDep.Smtp`. Neither is true: all three plain-dictionary caches are read\nwith a bare TryGetValue and never consult RecheckHelper, and all nine\nRemove*Entries methods have had no callers since 5277d94 unified rechecks onto\nthe AsyncLocal bypass. So `--recheck` on a Postmaster or Abuse finding re-runs\nthe check and reuses the cached RCPT verdict, and the same applies to relay\nunder an SMTP recheck. Pre-existing, not from this rework. Now documented as a\nknown gap with its practical effect, rather than closed — closing it is a\nbehaviour change, not a docs correction.\n\nSmaller corrections: architecture.md described merge-on-save, a domain-results\nsemaphore, on-demand flushes and a RequestFlush that no longer exist;\ndeployment.md still had a flush-on-completion in the job lifecycle;\nhorizontal-scaling.md still said pods republish \"their disk tier\" into Redis,\nfrom before the switch to memory; service-layer.md gained the write bags and\nthe two limitations of the plain dictionaries; the L2 key was documented\nwithout its InstanceName prefix while the epoch key had one; CacheManager's\nmethod list was missing WarmSharedCache and PruneSharedCacheIndex.\n\nVerified the type tags, CacheDep values and file-count arithmetic (13 at the\ndefaults, 145 at the retention floor, 49 at 24h/30min) against the code, and\nscanned every mermaid block for unbalanced fences and unescaped angle brackets.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01611PR4pvQSGB1t9K3hNAwE",
