2026-07-27T01:12:52.7279415Z               "id": "477cb0e238a0043365cadad129587ee60ee600af",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7285860Z               "message": "Stop keeping the shared-cache key index when nothing will use it\n\nThe key -> expiry index that lets an instance republish its L1 into Redis is\npruned by exactly one thing: the shared-cache watch tick. It was allocated\nwhenever Redis was configured, so `SharedCacheWatchSeconds=0` left it growing\nto every distinct key the process had ever cached -- expired ones included --\nfor the life of the process, with nothing ever reading it. Independent of\n`CacheDir`, so it bit the Redis-plus-watch-disabled combination specifically.\n\nGate the index on warming being possible at all rather than on Redis alone: a\n`warmSharedCache` flag threaded from Program.cs through the three probe\nservices, mirroring `persistToDisk`. With the watch off the index simply does\nnot exist. Write-through to the L2 is untouched -- only the ability to\nrepublish it later goes away, which is what the setting already meant.\n\nThe startup warning now says the index is dropped too, so re-enabling the\nwatch needs a restart.\n\n`SharedCacheIndexCount` is surfaced on the probe services and CacheManager so\nthe wiring is assertable rather than merely intended; both new web tests fail\nif the flag stops reaching the services.\n\nVerified live: with the watch on, an emptied Redis is still repopulated from\nmemory (169 entries, with and without a disk tier). With it off, validation,\nL2 write-through and a second cached validation all behave as before, nothing\nrepublishes, and the warning is emitted.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01611PR4pvQSGB1t9K3hNAwE",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7292740Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-26T09:44:53Z",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7293092Z               "tree_id": "2ca7106dd62245e1deb26df6f70ca4f4f451673c",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7293607Z               "url": "https://github.com/pkinerd/ednsv/commit/477cb0e238a0043365cadad129587ee60ee600af"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7294046Z             },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7294232Z             {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7294416Z               "author": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7294657Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7294935Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7295172Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7295401Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7295589Z               "committer": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7295831Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7296102Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7296337Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7296563Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7296754Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7297028Z               "id": "9fe6a56360658a79c4c89c874a7e31db6bee1983",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7305063Z               "message": "Remove the legacy per-type disk cache format\n\nThe one-file-per-cache-type layout (dns-queries.json, smtp-probes.json, and\nthe per-instance variants) existed only so an upgrade would not cold-start.\nNothing writes it, no deployment still holds one, and reading it cost a whole\nsecond loader: LoadLegacyAsync, LoadFileAsync, the domain-results reader,\nEnumerateVariants, the twelve filename constants, the mtime half of the sweep,\nand eleven Import*Cache methods on the probe services with no other callers.\n\nGone with it: ICacheEntry and the CachedAtUtc property it mandated. The record\nenvelope already carries the fetch time and expiry, so a timestamp inside the\npayload was a second copy that could disagree; DnsCacheEntry.CachedAtUtc was\nnever even assigned, so every DNS record on disk carried a default DateTime for\nnothing. The four DTOs only the legacy files used (PortProbe, Unreachable,\nAxfr, Ptr) go too; the five the record payloads and the Redis L2 share stay.\n\nAlso deletes the nine Remove*Entries methods on the three services. They lost\ntheir last caller when CLI and web rechecks unified onto the AsyncLocal\nbypass, and they had been broken since the write bag replaced the export log:\nthey iterate the bag, which holds only what has yet to be flushed rather than\neverything cached.\n\nThe sweep is now narrower as well as smaller — it only ever considers *.jsonl\ninside an instance folder, so pointing CacheDir at a shared location cannot\nput an operator's own files at risk.\n\nDocs: the legacy-format section is gone, along with the obituaries for the\nremoved cache-flush and cache-clear endpoints and the CLI's deleted\nentry-deletion recheck path. Documenting what no longer exists helps nobody.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01611PR4pvQSGB1t9K3hNAwE",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7312043Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-26T10:01:31Z",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7312378Z               "tree_id": "304a162e2e4a25be1cd0046b32a552b99ec9d9ee",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7312886Z               "url": "https://github.com/pkinerd/ednsv/commit/9fe6a56360658a79c4c89c874a7e31db6bee1983"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7313333Z             },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7313684Z             {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7313870Z               "author": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7314112Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7314391Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7314627Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7314859Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7315051Z               "committer": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7315295Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7315681Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7315912Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7316140Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7316329Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7316606Z               "id": "d4cad3fa32e519c759b5e71db521da4ae67870df",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7333885Z               "message": "Give every cache a TTL, not just the ProbeCaches\n\nSeven caches were plain ConcurrentDictionary fields with no expiry at all, so\nan entry lived for the life of the process and the working set grew with every\ndistinct key the process had ever seen: RCPT probes, relay tests, AXFR\nverdicts, AXFR responses, unreachable-server counts, domain result summaries,\nand the per-server LookupClient pool. The two AXFR caches were the expensive\nones — a zone transfer response is the largest thing this code holds, kept per\n(nameserver, domain) — and _serverClients held a client, and its socket pool,\nper nameserver IP ever queried.\n\nNew ExpiringMap<K,V>: a ConcurrentDictionary of value-plus-expiry. Not a\nMemoryCache, because three of these callers need something MemoryCache does not\noffer — an atomic read-modify-write for the unreachable counter, a tuple key\nfor the AXFR caches, and enumeration of the live set for the domain summaries.\n\nTwo mechanisms doing two jobs. Expiry is enforced on read, so the TTL holds\nexactly whether or not anything has swept; the read-side eviction matches the\nwhole entry, so a fresher value written in between survives. A prune every 256\nwrites bounds memory for keys written once and never read again — tied to\nwrites rather than a timer, because a map nobody writes to cannot be growing.\n\nBehaviour that needed care rather than a search-and-replace:\n\n- TryAdd treats an expired entry as absent and replaces it. A bare TryAdd\n  would report \"already present\" for a dead entry, so the refetched value would\n  be handed to the caller and then dropped — every call refetching for ever.\n- Imports keep the expiry stamped on their record instead of getting a fresh\n  full TTL, so a nearly-dead entry is not resurrected, and one already past its\n  expiry is refused. Threaded through TryImportRecord for rcpt, relay, axfr,\n  unreachable and domain-results.\n- AddOrUpdate restarts from the add value when the existing entry has expired,\n  so an unreachable count that aged out does not carry on from a value nobody\n  was allowed to read.\n- A null TTL (CacheTtlHours=0) means no expiry, exactly as everywhere else.\n\nCacheManager.PreviousResults had no callers and could not survive the change;\nreplaced by DomainResultCount. Per-map counts are exposed on the services for\nthe same reason the shared-cache index count is: it makes \"this does not grow\"\nassertable.\n\nTests: ExpiringMapTests covers the semantics (20 cases; five mutants of the\nexpiry logic each kill at least one). ServiceCacheExpiryTests covers the\nwiring — every map expires, imports honour their record's expiry, and a\nstructural test fails if a seventh plain dictionary ever appears. One test is\ndeliberately white-box: the live write paths into these maps all need the\nnetwork, so reading the TTL field is the only way to pin all seven, including\nthe AXFR response cache, which cannot be populated without a real zone\ntransfer. Each of the seven wirings was mutation-checked.\n\nVerified live: two instances on a shared mount still hand off correctly (169\nrecords written, 168 loaded, 2 rewritten), Redis still warms from disk, and\nCacheTtlHours=0 runs two validations with the second served from cache.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01611PR4pvQSGB1t9K3hNAwE",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7346290Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-26T10:21:35Z",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7346636Z               "tree_id": "e3080c38ba48e99990992377c8e8046dc0bfa8fc",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7347154Z               "url": "https://github.com/pkinerd/ednsv/commit/d4cad3fa32e519c759b5e71db521da4ae67870df"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7347611Z             },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7347999Z             {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7348400Z               "author": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7348698Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
