2026-07-27T01:12:52.7349225Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7349467Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7349663Z               "committer": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7349906Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7350179Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7350413Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7350640Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7350829Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7351098Z               "id": "607c0858d7199a6e145961916b17aec25ca97643",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7353187Z               "message": "Drop the last of the docs archaeology\n\nThree passages described how the code used to work rather than how it works:\nSaveDomainResult's former per-validation file write, the CacheTtlHours default\nthat \"was 24\", and the unreachable-server map's silence about expiry now that\nit has one. Rewritten to state current behaviour and keep only the reasoning a\nreader needs to avoid re-introducing what was removed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01611PR4pvQSGB1t9K3hNAwE",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7355249Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-26T10:25:32Z",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7355577Z               "tree_id": "7502990753285dfa5269454a725a505eb64e549b",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7356078Z               "url": "https://github.com/pkinerd/ednsv/commit/607c0858d7199a6e145961916b17aec25ca97643"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7356511Z             },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7356694Z             {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7356875Z               "author": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7357115Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7357400Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7357644Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7357878Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7358088Z               "committer": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7358549Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7358823Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7359058Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7359286Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7359475Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7359763Z               "id": "ddb29de86d7e08a70a16a5c3b1bd2d4adb6edd59",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7372145Z               "message": "Make the recheck bypass reach the caches it already named\n\nFour defects, one shape: a recheck that could not refetch.\n\n- CacheDep.Rcpt existed, RecheckHelper mapped the Postmaster and Abuse\n  categories to it, and nothing anywhere read it. Rechecking either finding\n  re-ran the check against the same cached verdict — a dead flag.\n- The relay verdict had no read gate. Its category (SMTP) declares CacheDep.Smtp\n  and that refreshed the handshake sitting right beside it, so a recheck\n  re-probed the connection and reused the relay answer.\n- The AXFR caches had no flag at all. CheckCategory.ZoneTransfer declared only\n  CacheDep.Dns, which names neither the verdict nor the transfer behind it.\n- The unreachable-server breaker is not a cache read, but it sits in front of\n  the server-query cache and returns EmptyResponse without asking anything. A\n  recheck run straight after the failure that prompted it lands inside the\n  five-minute decay window, so every server that had just failed was skipped\n  rather than retried and the bypass behind it never got to matter.\n\nExpiringMap.TryGetValue now takes the same CacheDep argument as\nProbeCache.TryGet, so the mechanism reads identically at both kinds of call\nsite. New CacheDep.Axfr, mapped from ZoneTransfer: its own bit rather than part\nof Dns, which nearly every category declares — sharing it would make every\nrecheck of anything re-run zone transfers against every nameserver.\n\nThe write side needed the same fix. These caches wrote add-if-absent, which is\ncorrect only while entries cannot be replaced: after a bypassed read the fresh\nanswer would find the stale entry still there, be returned to the caller and\ndropped — every recheck paying for a probe and changing nothing, for ever. They\nwrite unconditionally now, to the write bag as well as memory so the refreshed\nverdict reaches disk too.\n\nOne deliberate exception: a zone transfer that never happened is not recorded.\nEmptyResponse means the TCP attempt failed, which is indistinguishable from a\nrefused transfer once reduced to a bool, so caching it would record \"not\nvulnerable\" for a server nobody reached — and on a recheck would overwrite a\nreal finding with it. Same intent as shouldPersist everywhere else.\n\nTests: 13 cases covering each bypass, its blast radius (a DNS recheck must not\nre-probe RCPT), the replace-on-refetch behaviour, and the write bag. Ten\nmutants of the fix each kill at least one. Two need a definitive answer rather\nthan a refused connection, so they drive a stub SMTP server; SmtpProbeService\ngained an smtpPort parameter for that, defaulting to 25. Binding 25 itself was\nthe first attempt and was wrong: the suite runs classes in parallel and the\nstub started answering another test's localhost probes, which is exactly the\nflake an ephemeral port avoids.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01611PR4pvQSGB1t9K3hNAwE",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7383004Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-26T10:55:55Z",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7383350Z               "tree_id": "d0887a14a1bb9dbbd43e516d8459a00cdaa4faf0",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7383863Z               "url": "https://github.com/pkinerd/ednsv/commit/ddb29de86d7e08a70a16a5c3b1bd2d4adb6edd59"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7384304Z             },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7384485Z             {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7384666Z               "author": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7384903Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7385196Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7385435Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7385669Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7385909Z               "committer": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7386264Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7386749Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7387099Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7387422Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7402974Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7403494Z               "id": "a01ec1d48f2473d470029d89a2328904f5496320",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7416302Z               "message": "Run the ExpiringMap sweep off the caller's thread, paced by time not writes\n\nThe prune was triggered every 256 writes and executed inline, on whichever\nthread happened to complete that write — a probe thread coming back from a\nnetwork round-trip, made to walk a few thousand entries before it could return.\nIt was also unguarded: two threads could cross the threshold together and start\noverlapping scans.\n\nChecked what the platform does rather than guessing, since this is MemoryCache's\nproblem too. On .NET 8 it triggers the scan from cache operations\n(StartScanForExpiredItemsIfNeeded), rate-limits it by ExpirationScanFrequency —\none minute by default — and hands ScanForExpiredItems to TaskScheduler.Default.\nVerified by observation, not memory: with a 50ms scan frequency and expired\nentries, the post-eviction callback runs on a different managed thread from the\none that touched the cache, and 50 expired entries stay allocated until\nsomething does.\n\nSo the trigger was already the platform's own answer; the rest was not. Now:\nrate-limited to one sweep a minute instead of one per 256 writes, dispatched\nwith ThreadPool.UnsafeQueueUserWorkItem — Unsafe because a sweep has no business\ninheriting the recheck flags or trace sink of whichever validation happened to\ntrigger it — and single-flighted through a CompareExchange gate.\n\nHonest about that last one: the interval check alone closes the window so\ntightly that removing the gate leaves every test green, including one that\nreleases 64 threads simultaneously, and MemoryCache ships with the same race and\nno gate. It stays because it makes single-flight a guarantee rather than a\nnear-certainty, and because it is the only thing covering a scan that outlives\nits own interval. Both the code comment and the test say so rather than implying\nthe test proves it.\n\nAlso corrected a claim in the class doc: tuple keys are not a reason to avoid\nMemoryCache. Its keys are `object` and a ValueTuple round-trips fine — verified.\nThe two reasons that do hold are the atomic read-modify-write for the\nunreachable-server counter and enumeration of the live set for the domain\nsummaries, since MemoryCache exposes only Count until .NET 9.\n\nTests: the sweep is asynchronous now, so they wait for it rather than asserting\ninline. Added coverage that it does not run on the caller's thread, that a burst\nof concurrent writes costs one sweep rather than 64, that the pacing is the\ninterval and not the write count, that the gate is released so later sweeps\nstill run, and that an idle map is never swept at all. PruneRuns exists to make\nthose assertable. Two earlier attempts were my own bugs, not the code's: a\n64-task barrier starved the thread pool into a 58-second test, and short TTLs\nexpired the entries the assertions were about.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01611PR4pvQSGB1t9K3hNAwE",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7426888Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-26T11:16:24Z",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7427238Z               "tree_id": "c6a0a5aff3dc48c2d60e9ec6c9b0af27b7995eac",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7427749Z               "url": "https://github.com/pkinerd/ednsv/commit/a01ec1d48f2473d470029d89a2328904f5496320"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7428300Z             },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7428483Z             {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7428675Z               "author": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7428918Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7429202Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7429438Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7429671Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7429860Z               "committer": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7430101Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7430367Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7430599Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7430823Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7431011Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7431288Z               "id": "3ee3f5ef7a8cf327c6427880e2b1f49331048ee6",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7441339Z               "message": "Put ExpiringMap on MemoryCache instead of reimplementing expiry\n\nYou were right to push twice. I justified a hand-rolled ConcurrentDictionary of\nvalue-plus-expiry — with its own sweep, rate limit, single-flight gate and\nthread-pool dispatch — on three things MemoryCache supposedly could not do. Two\ndid not survive checking:\n\n- Non-string keys, for the (ip, domain) AXFR caches. Wrong: MemoryCache keys are\n  object and a ValueTuple compares structurally. Verified, not assumed.\n- Atomic read-modify-write, for the unreachable-server counter. Real, and it is\n  one call site, on a path only reached once a DNS query has already failed. A\n  lock covers it.\n- Enumeration of the live set, for the domain summaries. Real before .NET 9 —\n  and it had no production caller at all. Only tests read it, and they read\n  better through TryGet, which is what production actually uses.\n\nSo expiry is the platform's job again: exact on read, swept by MemoryCache on\nits own schedule. ~90 lines of maintenance code and the entire question of\nwhether our background work is guarded, off-thread and rate-limited go with it.\n\nWhat is left is the reason a wrapper exists at all — three rules that are this\nproject's rather than the platform's, each of which has been a bug when it lived\nat the call sites: the recheck bypass, a null TTL meaning no expiry\n(CacheTtlHours=0), and an import keeping the expiry stamped on its record. All\nthree are mutation-checked. A fourth property, \"an expired entry counts as\nabsent\", now has no code of ours to break — it is MemoryCache's behaviour.\n\nTwo things the rewrite exposed, both worth having:\n\n- TryAdd stopped being atomic when it became check-then-store, which would let\n  an import overwrite a value fetched from the network while the load ran. Now\n  under the same lock as AddOrUpdate. Reads and Set stay lock-free.\n- Count now means what MemoryCache means by it — entries held, including expired\n  ones not yet swept. That is the memory question, and it is the honest one to\n  expose; liveness is a question for a read. The service-level counters and the\n  expiry tests are corrected accordingly, and the tests are better for it: they\n  now assert that a stale RCPT verdict is actually refetched, not that a counter\n  went to zero.\n\nNet 258 lines lighter across source and tests.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01611PR4pvQSGB1t9K3hNAwE",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7450303Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-26T11:52:34Z",
