2026-07-27T01:12:52.7454309Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7454584Z               "id": "e0539fa42cfddfceae479fa57b9d05231ee57de3",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7462596Z               "message": "Remove the scaffolding the bespoke-expiry detour left behind\n\nThe mechanism was gone; its support surface was not. Five public members that\nnothing reads any more, all added to make the hand-rolled version's tests\nassert what they needed:\n\n- SmtpProbeService.RelayCacheCount\n- DnsResolverService.AxfrResponseCacheCount, ServerClientCount\n- CacheManager.DomainResultCount and DomainResultStore.Count\n\nThe tests that used them now assert through a read — that a stale RCPT verdict\nis actually refetched, that an expired AXFR verdict causes another transfer\nattempt — which is the contract rather than the bookkeeping. The three counters\nstill referenced stay: they carry the one assertion a read cannot make, that a\npast-expiry import stores nothing at all.\n\nAlso removed the archaeology that had accumulated in ExpiringMap's own class\ndoc and in the caching document: a table of the three things I had claimed\nMemoryCache could not do and what each turned out to be. That is a record of my\nwrong turn, not documentation — exactly what we stripped from the docs earlier\nfor the same reason. What the type is and why it exists at all stays.\n\nAnd DiskCacheService.EffectiveRetention was public with no caller outside the\nclass; now private. Found by the same sweep, unrelated to the detour.\n\nAccounting, since the question was whether the detour was actually unwound:\nExpiringMap.cs and its tests were 481 lines when first written over a\nConcurrentDictionary, 706 after the sweep rework, and are 451 now — smaller\nthan before the detour, and 78 lines of code to 76 of comment. Nothing named\nPruneRuns, AllocatedCount, SchedulePruneIfDue, PruneAndRelease, Snapshot or\npruneInterval survives anywhere in src, tests or docs.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01611PR4pvQSGB1t9K3hNAwE",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7469838Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-26T12:00:39Z",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7470185Z               "tree_id": "33e706cbbfd40e1173a605e31adbba1adba8ce13",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7470715Z               "url": "https://github.com/pkinerd/ednsv/commit/e0539fa42cfddfceae479fa57b9d05231ee57de3"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7471163Z             },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7471341Z             {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7471522Z               "author": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7471767Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7472050Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7472286Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7472515Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7472705Z               "committer": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7472954Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7473224Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7473452Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7473678Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7473866Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7474148Z               "id": "db8d4bd0e6af19d2cb8f2450bb3e1e5f3c4f6bf6",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7483413Z               "message": "Document running the shared store without a managed Redis\n\nMulti-pod deployments need a shared store, and the docs assumed a managed one.\nThey also never said how little it has to be: nothing in it is durable, so a\nsingle pod with persistence and replication switched off covers the whole job.\n\ndocs/self-hosted-redis.md sets out what actually lives there and what losing it\ncosts (in-flight jobs resubmit, the cache refills itself, config falls back to\nfile reads), then draws the operational conclusions from that: no AOF, no RDB,\nno replica, no backups, and allkeys-lru eviction is safe because every entry is\nreproducible.\n\nSizing is measured rather than guessed — one domain costs 150-300 keys and\n75-150 KB against a ~1 MB empty baseline, so 256 MB covers roughly a thousand\ndistinct domains per TTL window. The Kubernetes manifest, the NetworkPolicy that\nmakes running without a password acceptable, and the Compose equivalent are all\nincluded, with the flags verified against a real server: save disabled,\nappendonly no, maxmemory 256mb, allkeys-lru, plus a full validation through an\ninstance configured exactly as documented.\n\nOn which server: Redis and Valkey are named as the well-trodden self-hosting\nchoices, with the others noted as viable but less travelled. Licensing is left\nto operators deliberately — the terms have moved more than once, so the doc says\nto check the project's own site rather than trusting any summary of it. What the\napplication asks of the server is small enough that the decision is a policy\nquestion, not a technical one.\n\nTwo things that only this repository can offer, so they are written down: the\nfour live-Redis test classes are a ready-made compatibility harness on port\n6380, with the config/user compare-and-set flagged as the operation most likely\nto be missing from a partial RESP implementation; and a short note on why there\nis no first-party coordinator, so the question does not have to be re-derived.\n\nLinked from the README index, horizontal-scaling.md and configuration.md.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01611PR4pvQSGB1t9K3hNAwE",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7491977Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-26T12:51:07Z",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7492318Z               "tree_id": "514328df20d78d425234cc4e611c8c02b1de92f3",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7492859Z               "url": "https://github.com/pkinerd/ednsv/commit/db8d4bd0e6af19d2cb8f2450bb3e1e5f3c4f6bf6"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7493316Z             },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7493503Z             {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7493856Z               "author": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7494104Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7494396Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7494636Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7494869Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7495064Z               "committer": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7495309Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7495582Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7495818Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7496048Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7496233Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7496507Z               "id": "e6ba77a630841238a07b2111bc8f1690554a5e42",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7503721Z               "message": "Record measured compatibility for Valkey and Garnet\n\nThe doc told operators to check a substitute server against the live-Redis test\nclasses, then left the checking to them. Ran it instead.\n\nRedis 7.0.15, Valkey 7.2.13 and Garnet 2.1.0 are identical on every measure:\n31/31 harness tests, the beacon compare-and-set honoured, a full validation\nstoring 162 keys, and 169 republished after a FLUSHALL — that last one proving\nthe epoch nonce, the flush detection and the re-warm all work on each.\n\nThe CAS was checked at protocol level rather than inferred from the harness,\nbecause the test that covers rejection could have been satisfied by the\nfile-level If-Match instead of the transaction. A stale Condition.StringEqual\nmust be rejected and a correct one must commit; all three do, so config and user\nwrites are safe on any of them.\n\nThat makes my hedge about Garnet wrong, and the doc now says so: it passes\neverything, is MIT and written in .NET, and what the less common servers actually\ncost is operational familiarity rather than compatibility.\n\nAlso recorded what was not measured — sustained load, the maxmemory boundary,\nTLS, cluster mode — since none of it was, and the table would otherwise imply\nmore than it earned.\n\nOne process note for anyone repeating this: verify which server is answering\nbefore trusting a result. My first Redis baseline was actually Valkey still\nholding the port while redis-server failed to bind silently, and the reported\nredis_version looked plausible either way. `server_name` and `executable` from\nINFO are what distinguish them.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01611PR4pvQSGB1t9K3hNAwE",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7509961Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-26T12:58:04Z",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7510295Z               "tree_id": "1ad6f3ea6dc777264eb52a8138f4e3358ba3aac4",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7510787Z               "url": "https://github.com/pkinerd/ednsv/commit/e6ba77a630841238a07b2111bc8f1690554a5e42"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7511216Z             },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7511389Z             {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7511568Z               "author": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7511805Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7512084Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7512314Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7512545Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7512739Z               "committer": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7512980Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7513249Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7513477Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7513702Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7514015Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7514292Z               "id": "6e88af723653b1eb13c097ea859cadd756c7954f",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7523224Z               "message": "Recommend volatile-lru: the coordination keys must never be evictable\n\nThe doc said allkeys-lru and called eviction \"safe\". It is not. Three keys carry\nthe cross-pod coordination state — config:head, users:head and cache-epoch —\nand they are written without a TTL precisely so they do not disappear on their\nown. Under allkeys-* they are candidates like anything else.\n\nWorse than merely eligible: their access pattern is what an eviction policy\nhunts. The epoch nonce is read once every thirty seconds and written almost\nnever; the beacons are read on a memoised path and written only when a human\nchanges something. Against thousands of cache keys touched continuously during\nvalidations they are permanently the least-recently- and least-frequently-used\nkeys in the keyspace, so LRU and especially LFU select them first.\n\nMeasured on Redis 7.0.15 at maxmemory 3mb, filling with TTL'd entries:\nallkeys-lru evicted both coordination keys while 3,782 cache keys survived;\nvolatile-lru evicted 26,415 keys, every one of them a cache entry. Repeated with\nthe application itself under pressure: 26,327 evicted, both keys intact, still\nserving.\n\nThe cost of losing them is quiet rather than loud, which is why it was easy to\nmiss. A pod that finds a beacon missing republishes its own head and carries on\nwithout reloading from disk, so it can serve stale config indefinitely — or miss\na token revocation — until the next real change moves the beacon.\n\nThe doc now walks every maxmemory-policy rather than naming one: volatile-ttl is\ncalled out for evicting job keys first (they have the shortest TTLs, and an\nevicted job is the one thing users notice), and noeviction for degrading\nsilently since cache write-through is fire-and-forget.\n\nOne prerequisite is now explicit: volatile-lru needs something volatile, and\nCacheTtlHours=0 writes cache entries with no TTL. Measured, that combination\nrejected 15,274 of 20,000 writes with OOM.\n\nNo code change — the keys are already correctly persistent. It was the advice\nthat was wrong.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01611PR4pvQSGB1t9K3hNAwE",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7531513Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-26T14:58:17Z",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7531852Z               "tree_id": "0ef999e46bb01263385e64a7a716049a9a649e67",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7532370Z               "url": "https://github.com/pkinerd/ednsv/commit/6e88af723653b1eb13c097ea859cadd756c7954f"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7532807Z             },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7532986Z             {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7533170Z               "author": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7533410Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7533688Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7533926Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7534168Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7534361Z               "committer": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7534603Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7534871Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7535105Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7535331Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7535519Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7535798Z               "id": "8aec55c34eafcc475aa08c611bc802828593b885",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7541094Z               "message": "Document the shipped defaults, and that setting maxmemory is what creates the risk\n\nVerified against Redis 7.0.15 and Valkey 7.2.13: maxmemory defaults to 0, the\npolicy to noeviction, and — easy to miss — save defaults to RDB snapshots on,\nwhich is why the manifests pass --save \"\".\n\nThat reframes the previous commit. The out-of-the-box failure mode is not\neviction at all but an OOMKill: nothing is ever evicted, the server grows until\nthe container limit kills the pod, and every key goes including the coordination\nones. Setting maxmemory is what converts that into graceful eviction, and it is\nalso what introduces the hazard the eviction section describes. The default\nwould never have evicted a coordination key because it never evicts anything.\n\nAlso recorded that Garnet implements neither maxmemory nor maxmemory-policy — it\nbounds memory through its own options — so the eviction guidance does not carry\nacross to it. Sharpens what the less common servers actually cost: not wire\ncompatibility, which it has, but transferable operational knowledge.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01611PR4pvQSGB1t9K3hNAwE",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7545609Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-26T20:53:17Z",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7545931Z               "tree_id": "d079e7776f391a9597fa4e174a96d19da1ae6fd5",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7546431Z               "url": "https://github.com/pkinerd/ednsv/commit/8aec55c34eafcc475aa08c611bc802828593b885"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7546867Z             },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7547042Z             {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7547222Z               "author": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7547455Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7547724Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7547955Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7548365Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7548575Z               "committer": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7548834Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7549098Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7549322Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7549544Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7549735Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7550003Z               "id": "9012533888b63a2e30bc7d7b70f133be52c53278",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7557855Z               "message": "Give shared-cache keys a lifetime even when expiry is switched off\n\nCacheTtlHours=0 wrote Redis keys with no TTL at all, and the re-warm path wrote\nthe same entries with a 7,973-year one — 251,617,201,224 seconds, because an L1\nentry that never expires carries DateTime.MaxValue and its \"remaining life\" was\npassed through unexamined. So the same logical entry got a different lifetime\ndepending on which code path published it, and a keyspace could be persistent or\nquasi-persistent depending on whether a flush had happened yet.\n\nBoth are now bounded by DiskCacheService.UncappedRetention, the 24-hour floor the\ndisk tier has used since it became append-only. The reasoning transfers exactly:\n\"do not expire\" is safe for memory, which is keyed and therefore bounded by\ndistinct domains, and unsafe for any tier that is a fixed allocation. Redis is\nthe more constrained of the two.\n\nThe operational consequence is the part worth having. A key with no TTL is\ninvisible to a volatile-* eviction policy, so writing them there did not just\ngrow the shared cache — it removed the server's ability to shed it. Measured\nbefore: 15,274 of 20,000 writes rejected with OOM under volatile-lru. After:\nkeys land with an 86,394-second TTL, 26,334 evict cleanly under the same\npressure, zero write errors, both coordination keys intact, and the application\nstill serving.\n\nA floor, not a cap: CacheTtlHours=168 still puts a week on the Redis keys. Only\n\"no expiry at all\" is translated into something finite, so no existing\nconfiguration changes behaviour.\n\nNot made configurable. A separate max-Redis-TTL knob would exist to bound memory,\nand maxmemory with volatile-lru already does that properly — now that there is\nalways something volatile to evict. The disk equivalent is a constant for the\nsame reason.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01611PR4pvQSGB1t9K3hNAwE",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7564857Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-26T21:07:19Z",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7565300Z               "tree_id": "ec1665950bc5f93f0015b9e620a898f168547a77",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7565796Z               "url": "https://github.com/pkinerd/ednsv/commit/9012533888b63a2e30bc7d7b70f133be52c53278"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7566229Z             },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7566407Z             {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7566587Z               "author": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7566821Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7567095Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7567326Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7567669Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7567855Z               "committer": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7568090Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7568742Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7568982Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7569212Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7569397Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7569666Z               "id": "24d01cb5d7d3a4db7c0b3640d2545f5170cec9e9",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7577085Z               "message": "Pin that the coordination keys stay persistent\n\nAnswering \"the floor obviously doesn't apply to the keys we're preserving?\" —\ncorrect, and now asserted rather than assumed. The floor lives in ProbeCacheL2\nand only its own writes call it; config:head, users:head and cache-epoch are\nwritten directly by ConfigService, AuthService and SharedCacheEpoch with no\nexpiry argument at all.\n\nMeasured with CacheTtlHours=0, which is the case that would expose a leak:\ndb0:keys=162,expires=160 — every cache entry evictable, both coordination keys\npersistent and therefore invisible to a volatile-* policy, which is the whole\npoint.\n\nThe test covers both beacon writes that actually run, plus the epoch nonce, and\neach is mutation-checked by giving it a 24h TTL. Two of the three die.\n\nThree things this cost, all my own errors, all worth recording:\n\n- The first version asserted only KeyTimeToLive, which returns null both for \"no\n  TTL\" and for \"no key\". Against a key that had not been written yet it passed\n  vacuously. Existence is now asserted first.\n- The beacon is published lazily on first Snapshot(), not by the constructor, so\n  the original assertion ran before anything had written it. Confirmed with a\n  probe rather than reasoning about it.\n- A mutation that appeared to survive turned out to be a no-op — the replacement\n  had not matched. Mutations now assert the file actually changed.\n\nConfigService's third beacon write, in InitBeaconLocked, is deliberately left\nuncovered and the test says so: EnsureFresh publishes the key first, so that\nbranch only ever finds a beacon already present and adopts it. Giving it a TTL\nis a mutation nothing can kill, because the line never executes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01611PR4pvQSGB1t9K3hNAwE",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7583697Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-26T21:17:13Z",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7584030Z               "tree_id": "c3dabe7bc2bf0db5028bd139769ae2c5a135a185",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7584528Z               "url": "https://github.com/pkinerd/ednsv/commit/24d01cb5d7d3a4db7c0b3640d2545f5170cec9e9"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7584955Z             },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7585132Z             {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7585310Z               "author": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7585544Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7585821Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7586052Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7586282Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7586469Z               "committer": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7586705Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7586969Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7587203Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7587429Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7587614Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7588029Z               "id": "46521c1be21d2ef310945e40abbaccec86fe715d",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7595873Z               "message": "Never let a lost head beacon strand a pod on stale config or users\n\nThe config and users heads are opaque GUIDs, which is what makes losing the\nbeacon dangerous rather than merely inconvenient: a GUID carries no information\nabout the content it names, so a head published against unverified state\nsatisfies every subsequent \"has it changed?\" check forever.\n\nTwo paths did exactly that. A pod that found the beacon missing republished its\nown in-memory head without reading the file, so the first pod to notice a flush\nbecame the cluster's truth even when it was behind — every peer then converged\nonto a head that permanently looked current. And a pod that adopted a peer's\nhead recorded it even when the reload had failed, leaving it matching the beacon\nwhile serving the copy it had before. Reproduced end to end for config, and the\nsame shape in AuthService meant a revoked token could keep authenticating.\n\nBoth now hold to one rule: never claim a head you have not verified. Read the\nfile before republishing, and adopt a peer's head only on a successful read. A\nwrite that cannot verify the head fails its compare-and-set and reports an error\nrather than overwriting the peer's change.\n\nAdded a backstop for what a single evictable key structurally cannot express: a\nfreshness check that finds the head unchanged also compares the file against the\nversion the pod loaded. That covers the flush landing between a writer's CAS and\nits file write, and an operator editing the mount by hand.\n\nBeaconLossTests covers all of it against a live Redis. L2LifetimeTests now calls\nLoadOrSeed as real callers do, which incidentally brings InitBeaconLocked under\ntest — the branch its comment said could never execute.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01611PR4pvQSGB1t9K3hNAwE",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7602787Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-26T21:56:38Z",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7603118Z               "tree_id": "f0b98036e6b5cca1dc6ff1c1b499dabfba4189c8",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7603617Z               "url": "https://github.com/pkinerd/ednsv/commit/46521c1be21d2ef310945e40abbaccec86fe715d"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7604047Z             },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7604222Z             {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7604401Z               "author": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7604639Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7604911Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7605151Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7605381Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7605567Z               "committer": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7605804Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7606069Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7606299Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7606521Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7606704Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7606977Z               "id": "f3a0287fd5b39e0c41e35ee6586f8c9c0ef8225d",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7614264Z               "message": "Give the disk and Redis tiers their own \"no expiry\" floors\n\nBoth substitute 24 hours when CacheTtlHours=0, and until now both read it from\nDiskCacheService.UncappedRetention, whose comment claimed the L2 applied \"the\nsame floor for the same reason\". It is not the same reason.\n\nThe disk floor exists because every flush appends a file and nothing dedupes\nthem, so the sweep is the only thing that removes anything — and because with no\nTTL it is also the load's only staleness cutoff, so what it leaves is what gets\nserved after a restart. The Redis floor exists because the L2 is a fixed\nallocation shared by every pod and a key with no TTL is invisible to the\nvolatile-* policy the deployment guidance recommends, so writing without one\nremoves the server's ability to shed the cache rather than merely growing it.\n\nThe two are sized against different things and, in the recommended layouts, are\nnot even used together: a single instance runs the disk tier without Redis, and\na multi-pod deployment runs Redis with CacheDir=none. Sharing one constant meant\na change made for one tier would silently resize the other sevenfold — the disk\nnumber is bounded by file-count arithmetic, the Redis one by how much of a\nshared allocation a week of keys would occupy.\n\nSo ProbeCacheL2.UncappedLifetime now owns the L2's, with the reasoning that\nbelongs to it, and DiskCacheService.UncappedRetention documents itself as the\ndisk tier's and nothing else's. Same values, no shared edge. ProbeCache no\nlonger depends on DiskCacheService for policy at all.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01611PR4pvQSGB1t9K3hNAwE",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7620649Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-26T23:17:48Z",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7620981Z               "tree_id": "0fc0cdf71bff64c7bf8e14db6750bc1e1be757b1",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7621488Z               "url": "https://github.com/pkinerd/ednsv/commit/f3a0287fd5b39e0c41e35ee6586f8c9c0ef8225d"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7621919Z             },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7622095Z             {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7622277Z               "author": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7622515Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7622788Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7623020Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7623246Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7623433Z               "committer": {
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7623679Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7623945Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7624176Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7624402Z               },
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7624592Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7624867Z               "id": "3b8f2a3d141ba160bd9449b897dedcb77f7962bc",
2026-07-27T01:12:52.7629333Z               "message": "Say what CacheTtlHours=0 actually trades\n\nThe facts were all there — memory never expires, files are swept after 24h — but\nspread across three documents and never joined up. Joined up they are\ncounterintuitive: 0 reads as the longest retention and is the shortest durable\none, because both tiers that survive a restart are capped while an explicit\nsetting is honoured in full. A process restarting under 0 comes back with less\ncache than one restarting under 168.\n\nAdds the consequence and the resulting advice — 0 for a long-lived process that\nshould never re-fetch, a large explicit value for restarts that should stay warm\n— to caching-architecture's \"Turning expiry off\", and a pointer to it from the\ntwo settings tables. Also corrects both tables, which mentioned only the file\nsweep and not the Redis lifetime.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01611PR4pvQSGB1t9K3hNAwE",
